    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f991ee211e7ac12c685435affc3727c846b13dedc2343117bc1c1724e5cc4196",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The transferable random-matrix comparison is a family-indexed probability law, not a claim about every deterministic unitary polynomial. Arithmetic family structure selects a symmetry class and has a rigorous Frobenius/equidistribution realization over finite fields; in the classical model, deterministic failures may be negligible under the prescribed sampling law, while model adequacy can still be tested against irregular gaps and exceptional multiplicities that arithmetic L-functions are expected or known to exhibit.",
